index 34d27a8779bb2cf4ce1cda881ef552d3053618c1..32cb4474cb642ecee6318042b744eeec0567a90c 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -4,6 +4,14 @@ Each release's page on GitHub has its notes and measurements. See [the 1.0.0 rel
 
 ## Unreleased
 
+- Fix, Flash Next `--tp 2` with `TF_FLASHNEXT_TP_PLE_PEER=1`: rank 0, which skips the n-gram gather of a prompt pass, now moves its n-gram history past the pass's tokens as the gather would. Before, the two ranks' histories parted after the first such pass, so the next prompt-cache save or restore stopped with RankStateDiffers and rank 0's next decode rows gathered after the wrong tokens.
+
+- Opt-in, Flash Next shared verify passes: `TF_FLASHNEXT_VERIFY_PICK_BATCH=1` (with `TF_FLASHNEXT_SAMPLE_CAND=1`) takes the sampled windows' candidates from one launch over the pass's own score rows instead of one launch a window, with the same draws and replies.
+
+- Opt-in, Flash Next shared decode rounds: `TF_FLASHNEXT_DRAFT_PICK_BATCH=1` picks each draft head step's best and sampling candidates for all of the round's streams in one launch instead of one small launch a stream, with the same drafts and replies.
+
+- Opt-in, Flash Next prompt-cache marks (each agent turn keeps a state where its history ends, a few rows before the prompt's end): `TF_FLASHNEXT_MTP_CACHE_ONLY=1` runs the MTP head's prompt rows of a non-final chunk only as far as its cache writes; `TF_FLASHNEXT_SNAP_EARLY=1` allocates the kept state's buffer while the marked pass still runs (both ranks at `--tp 2`); `TF_FLASHNEXT_SNAP_SPLIT=1` copies the model's part of the state while the head still runs. These three give the same tokens and the same draft cache bytes. `TF_FLASHNEXT_MARK_IN_PASS=N` (1 to 512) captures a mark at most N rows before a pass's end inside that pass instead of running the last rows as a pass of their own; the kept state is the mark's, but the rows after it are computed as an unmarked prompt's, so replies may differ slightly. Unset, nothing changes.
+
 - Opt-in: `TENSORFOLD_PREFILL_VISIT_MS=N` ends a prompt fill pass once it has run N ms while a stream could decode, so decoding streams wait at most about N ms plus one prompt call for their next round when many short prompts arrive together. `TENSORFOLD_PREFILL_SHARE_SHORT=1` makes `TENSORFOLD_PREFILL_SHARE` bill those short-prompt bursts as it bills long prompt steps. Neither cuts a prompt into different calls, so replies are the same tokens; unset, nothing changes.
 
 - On Ryzen AI Max (RDNA 3.5) GPUs, one- and two-row decode launches use narrower stream tiles, so Qwen3.6-35B-A3B chat decodes about 18% faster with drafts on, and Qwen3.8-27B about 5% faster, with the same tokens (#1855). Thanks to @AbrahamCete.
